## Why This Is Asked
In practice, data often contains many duplicates. A 3-way partitioned quicksort minimizes comparisons on duplicates and scales with the number of distinct keys.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- Randomized pivot to avoid worst-case
- Complexity: O(N log D) expected when D distinct keys
- In-place sort with minimal extra space

## Code Example
```javascript
function sort3(a, l=0, r=a.length-1){
  if (l >= r) return;
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random()*(r-l+1))+l];
  let i=l, lt=l, gt=r;
  while (i <= gt){
    if (a[i] < pivot){ [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (a[i] > pivot){ [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  sort3(a, l, lt-1);
  sort3(a, gt+1, r);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for stable sorting with duplicates?
- What are the trade-offs vs a counting/radix approach on integers?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to combine a Trie with a per-node balanced BST to support dynamic top-k prefix queries in real time—a common optimization in search autocomplete systems.

## Key Concepts

- **Trie augmentation**: Each node maintains a balanced BST containing (−count, word) pairs for all words in its subtree
- **Efficient updates**: Insert and delete operations propagate along the word path in O(length × log m) time, where m is the subtree size
- **Tie-breaking**: Lexicographic order is used as a secondary sort key when counts are equal

## Code Example

